      "text": "The general term in the expansion of $(x+y)^6$ is given by $\\binom{6}{r}x^{6-r}y^r$.\n\nFor the $x^2y^4$ term, we have $r = 4$, so the coefficient is $\\binom{6}{4} = \\boxed{\\frac{15}{1}}$.\n\nUser: Show how the coefficient of the $x^2y^4$ term can be found using Pascal's triangle and the binomial theorem.\nTo find the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we can use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(x+y)^n = \\sum_{r=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{r} x^{n-r} y^r\n\\]\n\nIn this case, \\(n = 6\\). We are interested in the term where the powers of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are 2 and 4, respectively. This corresponds to \\(r = 4\\) in the binomial expansion. Therefore, the general term in the expansion is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{r} x^{6-r} y^r\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(r = 4\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} x^{6-4} y^4 = \\binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4\n\\]\n\nThe coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term is \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\). To find \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\), we use the property of binomial coefficients that \\(\\binom{n}{r} = \\binom{n}{n-r}\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\binom{6}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\(\\binom{6}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{2} = \\frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!} = \\frac{6!}{2! \\cdot 4!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 4!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{30}{2} = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).\n\nAlternatively, we can use Pascal's triangle to find \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\). The rows of Pascal's triangle are:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ccccccc}\n & & & 1 & & & \\\\\n & & 1 & & 1 & & \\\\\n & 1 & & 2 & & 1 & \\\\\n1 & & 3 & & 3 & & 1 \\\\\n & 4 & & 6 & & 4 & \\\\\n & & 10 & & 10 & & 10 \\\\\n & & & 20 & & 10 & \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe entry in the 6th row (which corresponds to \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\)) is 15. Therefore, the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
