      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we use the Binomial Theorem. The Binomial Theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nIn this case, \\(n = 6\\), so we have:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^{6} \\binom{6}{k} x^{6-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nWe are interested in the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term. This term corresponds to the term where \\(n-k = 2\\) and \\(k = 4\\), because \\(x^{6-2} = x^2\\) and \\(y^4\\).\n\nSo, we need to find the coefficient for the term where \\(k = 4\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} x^{6-4} y^4 = \\binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\frac{6!}{4!(6-4)!} = \\frac{6!}{4! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4!}{4! \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = 15\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
